WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.741 --> 00:00:10.042
My name is Neil Shubin, I'm from the University of Chicago.

00:00:10.043 --> 00:00:12.211
And I'm going to talk today about finding Tiktaalik.

00:00:12.212 --> 00:00:16.883
My life's quest began in graduate school, in the second year

00:00:16.884 --> 00:00:19.986
when I saw this exact slide. This is the slide my professor

00:00:19.987 --> 00:00:25.591
showed back in 1986, and it showed what we knew at the time as

00:00:25.592 --> 00:00:29.662
the fish to tetrapod transition, which is the transition from life in water

00:00:29.663 --> 00:00:33.432
to life on land, as seen by these cartoons of these fossil creatures.

00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:38.638
The one on top is a fish from 390 million years ago, the one on the bottom

00:00:38.639 --> 00:00:43.376
is a tetrapod from about 365 million years ago. I remember as a student

00:00:43.377 --> 00:00:46.712
seeing this diagram for the first time and thinking, "Holy cow!

00:00:46.713 --> 00:00:52.051
What a first class scientific problem!" How did fish evolve to walk on land? It

00:00:52.052 --> 00:00:54.987
seemed so utterly impossible go to from the thing on top to the thing

00:00:54.988 --> 00:00:58.057
on the bottom. And that's what became my quest. So like any

00:00:58.058 --> 00:01:01.360
good paleontologist, I applied the paleontological toolkit to try to find

00:01:01.361 --> 00:01:04.530
an intermediate, say some sort of creature between these two.

00:01:04.531 --> 00:01:08.668
And so what do we do when we try to find a new place to discover fossils?

00:01:08.669 --> 00:01:11.971
What we do is we look for places in the world that have three things.

00:01:11.972 --> 00:01:16.342
The first is rocks of the right age, you need rocks of the right age

00:01:16.343 --> 00:01:19.712
to answer whatever question interests you. So in this case, clearly

00:01:19.713 --> 00:01:24.250
we want something between 390 and 365 million years old.

00:01:24.251 --> 00:01:28.354
That's of a time period known as the Late Devonian. The other thing

00:01:28.355 --> 00:01:31.090
that was really important, of course, is to have rocks of the right type.

00:01:31.091 --> 00:01:35.294
Not every kind of rock holds fossils. Some are super heated, some are

00:01:35.295 --> 00:01:39.599
highly pressurized. So we needed a particular kind of rock, sedimentary

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:43.769
rocks, and not only that, sedimentary rocks in the right environment

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:47.573
to hold the kind of intermediates we're looking for. So what's the

00:01:47.574 --> 00:01:50.443
other variable? Rocks of the right age. Rocks of the right type.

00:01:50.444 --> 00:01:54.013
Well, it does me no good if my wonderful rocks of the right age

00:01:54.014 --> 00:01:57.383
and the right type are buried five miles underground, right? These things

00:01:57.384 --> 00:02:01.053
have to be exposed at the surface. So what we do is we look for places

00:02:01.054 --> 00:02:04.457
in the world where we have naked bedrock, okay? So that's it. If you

00:02:04.458 --> 00:02:07.727
ever want to run your own expedition someday, look for places in the world

00:02:07.728 --> 00:02:10.429
that have rocks of the right age, rocks of the right type, and rocks that

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:14.333
are exposed at the surface. There's another criterion, and in this case

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:19.338
was accessibility. And this is where I began my first scientific expeditions.

00:02:19.339 --> 00:02:24.544
These were accessible, because I had just begun my first academic

00:02:24.545 --> 00:02:28.147
job at the University of Pennsylvania, here at the southeastern corner

00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:31.651
of the state of Pennsylvania. And I dug out a geological map of

00:02:31.652 --> 00:02:35.855
the state. And I'm showing you here what is sort of relevant for our

00:02:35.856 --> 00:02:39.158
question. If you look at this map of Pennsylvania, what you see is

00:02:39.159 --> 00:02:43.896
everything in purple are Devonian age rocks. So I have lots of

00:02:43.897 --> 00:02:47.233
rocks of the right age throughout the state of Pennsylvania. But what about

00:02:47.234 --> 00:02:51.137
that second variable, rocks of the right type? Well if you want to

00:02:51.138 --> 00:02:55.141
think about what Pennsylvania looked like in the Devonian, get Pittsburg,

00:02:55.142 --> 00:03:00.646
Harrisburg, Philadelphia, out of your brain. And think amazon delta.

00:03:00.647 --> 00:03:04.984
This is a cartoon of what Pennsylvania looked like 365 million years ago.

00:03:04.985 --> 00:03:09.589
You had highlands to the east, an inland sea to the west, and a series

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:14.227
of rivers draining from east to west. Now if you're a paleontologist

00:03:14.228 --> 00:03:18.231
wanting to find fossils at the cusp of the transition from life in water

00:03:18.232 --> 00:03:22.835
to life on land, this is perfect. Because if you have the right exposures,

00:03:22.836 --> 00:03:27.707
you can sample ancient seaways, ancient rivers and streams, even ancient

00:03:27.708 --> 00:03:31.911
upland deposits. Now the problem is Pennsylvania doesn't have a whole lot

00:03:31.912 --> 00:03:36.415
of exposures. So that third variable is very tough. So my initial

00:03:36.416 --> 00:03:41.020
expeditions, if you will, became following the Pennsylvania Department of

00:03:41.021 --> 00:03:45.291
Transportation around as they build new roads. And if we were really lucky,

00:03:45.292 --> 00:03:48.995
what they would do is they would be digging a new road in a place of Devonian

00:03:48.996 --> 00:03:53.299
rock. And that's what you see here. This is a set of rocks about an hour north

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:57.937
of State College Pennsylvania. And this expanse extends about half a mile

00:03:57.938 --> 00:04:01.641
long. It's a big red hill of rock -- it's called Red Hill, no surprise.

00:04:01.642 --> 00:04:05.945
But what you see in these layers is the strata, the ancient layers

00:04:05.946 --> 00:04:10.316
of ancient streams. And this is really perfect. Almost as soon as we

00:04:10.317 --> 00:04:15.488
hit this site in the early '90s, we started to find fossils. The first thing

00:04:15.489 --> 00:04:19.759
we started to find were teeth the size of railroad spikes. So big monsters, almost the size of

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:23.162
your thumb. Then we started to find the jaws of these creatures. Here's the

00:04:23.163 --> 00:04:26.165
front end of one of these jaws. These jaws would be the length of your arm.

00:04:26.166 --> 00:04:29.702
Alright, so they're really big. These big animals are probably about

00:04:29.703 --> 00:04:33.306
16 feet long. Then we started to find all kinds of other fish, like

00:04:33.307 --> 00:04:37.109
this little mashed thing here. This is actually a mashed fossil

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:41.747
of a lobed fin fish, which is related to lungfish today. So that's a head

00:04:41.748 --> 00:04:45.518
and a body. But then my colleague, Ted Daeschler, who's been my partner

00:04:45.519 --> 00:04:49.655
in all these expeditions for the last number of decades. Ted found

00:04:49.656 --> 00:04:54.227
early tetrapods. One day I had left, one week I had left to do

00:04:54.228 --> 00:04:56.429
expeditions elsewhere, and he had just discovered a series of

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:59.832
bones. Arm bones, leg bones, from creatures that represented

00:04:59.833 --> 00:05:03.903
early limbed animals. And working with the National Geographic Society,

00:05:03.904 --> 00:05:07.840
Ted and I were able to reconstruct what this ancient road in Pennsylvania

00:05:07.841 --> 00:05:12.645
looked like 365 million years ago. I mean we had some of the first

00:05:12.646 --> 00:05:16.449
forests, you can see this, and ancient streams, see that in the middle

00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:21.254
here is that big monster fish, about 16 foot long fish. And then you see

00:05:21.255 --> 00:05:24.023
a series of other kinds of fish, in particular, you see the little creatures

00:05:24.024 --> 00:05:27.860
with limbs crawling around both on land and in the water.

00:05:27.861 --> 00:05:32.098
Now this was great, but Ted and I realized we had a problem.

00:05:32.099 --> 00:05:38.337
Why? We're in rocks about 365 million years old and we're already finding

00:05:38.338 --> 00:05:41.407
pretty advanced limbed creatures. Remember what motivated this

00:05:41.408 --> 00:05:45.077
whole quest was this whole slide I saw in graduate school.

00:05:45.078 --> 00:05:48.014
We're not finding intermediates, we're already finding pretty advanced

00:05:48.015 --> 00:05:50.950
limbed animals, I want something in the middle here. Well, to give you

00:05:50.951 --> 00:05:53.219
a sense of what we're after, let's just go through some of the

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:56.556
traits that we look for. When you look at the creature on top, it has a

00:05:56.557 --> 00:06:00.393
conical head with eyes on either side. The creature on the bottom

00:06:00.394 --> 00:06:04.297
has a flat head with eyes on top. So really one of the kind of features

00:06:04.298 --> 00:06:08.634
that changes in this transition is the shape of the head.

00:06:08.635 --> 00:06:11.270
The other thing that changes is if you look at the creature on top,

00:06:11.271 --> 00:06:15.074
the fish on top, you'll see the head is connected to the shoulder girdle by a series

00:06:15.075 --> 00:06:19.045
of bones. It doesn't have a neck. Fish don't have necks where the head

00:06:19.046 --> 00:06:23.149
can bend independently of the body. Early limbed animals, early

00:06:23.150 --> 00:06:26.919
tetrapods, have a neck where the head can swivel independently

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:32.158
and it can look around as its legs are on the ground. Finally, another big feature

00:06:32.159 --> 00:06:35.461
which obviously gives it its name, tetrapods, four-legged animal,

00:06:35.462 --> 00:06:39.498
is fish have fins and the earliest tetrapods have limbs with fingers

00:06:39.499 --> 00:06:43.669
and toes and wrists and ankles. We're finding a lot of fingers, toes, and wrists,

00:06:43.670 --> 00:06:46.873
and ankles, right? We're not finding any intermediate. What Ted and I

00:06:46.874 --> 00:06:51.010
are looking for is a flat headed fish with say fins or something in that

00:06:51.011 --> 00:06:55.481
intermediate zone. To get that, what we had to do was move back in time,

00:06:55.482 --> 00:07:00.620
back in time about 15 million years using analyses that other colleagues had done.

00:07:00.621 --> 00:07:05.124
So Ted and I pulled out the paleontological rule book and began looking for

00:07:05.125 --> 00:07:07.793
places that have rocks that are the right age, rocks that are the right type,

00:07:07.794 --> 00:07:11.964
and rocks that are exposed. Wed had an idea to work in Brazil.

00:07:11.965 --> 00:07:16.369
We had an idea to work in western North America. Then everything

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:21.374
changed one day in my office, back in the late '90s, when we had a

00:07:21.375 --> 00:07:24.944
geological debate. And to settle the debate, I pulled out a college

00:07:24.945 --> 00:07:29.081
geology textbook, this one right here. It's now in its eleventh edition.

00:07:29.082 --> 00:07:31.484
So that gives you a sense of how old I am. But anyway, so we

00:07:31.485 --> 00:07:35.721
finished the debate and I was paging through the book, and we ran

00:07:35.722 --> 00:07:40.293
into this figure. This is the figure that changed the traction of research

00:07:40.294 --> 00:07:45.498
for me, about 15 years ago. What it is is what we look for in a nutshell

00:07:45.499 --> 00:07:49.836
as paleontologist. You'll see the caption, it says upper Devonian sedimentary

00:07:49.837 --> 00:07:53.372
facies. Rocks more or less of the right age and the right type.

00:07:53.373 --> 00:07:57.076
But here you see a map of North America, and superimposed on that

00:07:57.077 --> 00:08:01.380
map is an interpretation of the geology of the Devonian age

00:08:01.381 --> 00:08:04.150
rocks. Now you'll notice in the western part of North America,

00:08:04.151 --> 00:08:08.654
there are Devonian rocks mapped as ancient oceans, but then the authors

00:08:08.655 --> 00:08:12.592
identify three different areas, shown in red here, that had rocks

00:08:12.593 --> 00:08:15.695
that had formed in ancient rivers and streams. Ancient rivers and stream

00:08:15.696 --> 00:08:20.533
environments, delta systems. The first one, you've seen before.

00:08:20.534 --> 00:08:23.769
That's the one I just showed you from Pennsylvania, that's the ongoing Catskill

00:08:23.770 --> 00:08:27.473
project that we're working on along the roadsides of Pennsylvania.

00:08:27.474 --> 00:08:31.544
There's another one on top here in East Greenland, that was well studied.

00:08:31.545 --> 00:08:35.481
That was discovered in the '20s by Danes and Swedes. And

00:08:35.482 --> 00:08:38.618
had produced a number of early tetrapods or limbed animals, in fact

00:08:38.619 --> 00:08:41.487
the cartoon I've been showing you is the cartoon derived from

00:08:41.488 --> 00:08:44.357
one of the fossils from this area. You see where I'm going?

00:08:44.358 --> 00:08:48.661
Extending 1500 kilometers east to west across the Canadian arctic,

00:08:48.662 --> 00:08:53.699
was mapped Devonian aged rock, completely unexplored. Not only that,

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:56.636
that rock was perfect. It was mapped as being from ancient rivers

00:08:56.637 --> 00:08:59.972
and streams, ancient delta systems. So Ted and I were really excited,

00:08:59.973 --> 00:09:03.142
and just a little anecdote here is, we were so excited. It's all happening

00:09:03.143 --> 00:09:06.312
one morning, so we ran to a Chinese food restaurant across the street

00:09:06.313 --> 00:09:10.349
and had a fortune cookie. And that fortune cookie said, "Soon you'll

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:13.619
be at the top of the world." So yes, here we go! We had a fortune cookie,

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:18.057
off we go! So anyway, this is a colorized version of one of the scientific papers

00:09:18.058 --> 00:09:21.794
and it shows the stratigraphic section right here, and as well as the

00:09:21.795 --> 00:09:26.065
map. And you can see, super-imposed over the map of the Canadian

00:09:26.066 --> 00:09:31.170
arctic is a huge area, shown in red here, of Devonian age rock

00:09:31.171 --> 00:09:36.342
So you can see it's all over. This is 1500 kilometers of section.

00:09:36.343 --> 00:09:40.346
So now we had a new problem, instead of driving in our station

00:09:40.347 --> 00:09:43.516
wagons to central Pennsylvania, we're now working several hundred

00:09:43.517 --> 00:09:46.652
miles from the North Pole, where the arrow is in this slide.

00:09:46.653 --> 00:09:50.790
To give you a sense, the nearest town is about 300 miles away.

00:09:50.791 --> 00:09:54.827
I'm going to show you a picture of that town, with about 170 permanent residents.

00:09:54.828 --> 00:09:59.332
This is a picture of that town in spring, Beresford, Canada. This is a big city!

00:09:59.333 --> 00:10:03.135
And so this is resupply. Anyway, as you can imagine, much of our

00:10:03.136 --> 00:10:07.273
supply is based on planes and helicopters. And so we're highly

00:10:07.274 --> 00:10:13.379
dependent on aircraft. Now the way this affects science, is that the supply chain

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:17.583
is very long. And fossils are rocks, they're very heavy. So that means we

00:10:17.584 --> 00:10:21.487
can't take home everything we find. So much of what we do in the field

00:10:21.488 --> 00:10:24.290
is trying to determine whether we have really good material that is

00:10:24.291 --> 00:10:31.531
worth spending the money to freight it home. So we started in 1998,

00:10:31.532 --> 00:10:35.701
we had that fortune cookie in 1998. Our first expedition was in 1999.

00:10:35.702 --> 00:10:38.371
It took us about a year to get the permits, the money, all that good stuff.

00:10:38.372 --> 00:10:41.841
And we started with great hope in the western part of the Canadian arctic.

00:10:41.842 --> 00:10:46.145
And you can see what that area looks like. It looks kind of flat, doesn't it?

00:10:46.146 --> 00:10:49.715
And you can see our camp just below me here. This is Devonian rock.

00:10:49.716 --> 00:10:52.485
So we'd wake up in the morning and essentially walk all over these

00:10:52.486 --> 00:10:56.722
Devonian rocks, looking for the bones that are weathering on the surface.

00:10:56.723 --> 00:10:59.592
We realized we had a big problem, this is the wrong part of the Arctic.

00:10:59.593 --> 00:11:03.396
We were in all these rocks, which were marine rocks formed in ancient

00:11:03.397 --> 00:11:07.066
oceans. It was pretty clear based on other people's discoveries

00:11:07.067 --> 00:11:11.904
that we needed to hit rocks in rivers and streams. So what that meant

00:11:11.905 --> 00:11:15.641
is using the geological map, this is a version of what I showed you

00:11:15.642 --> 00:11:19.478
before for Pennsylvania, it applied also to the arctic. We were in the

00:11:19.479 --> 00:11:23.082
ancient seaways, shown in blue. We had to move upstream. To do

00:11:23.083 --> 00:11:28.454
that geologically, it meant moving east. So we retooled, the next year we went back

00:11:28.455 --> 00:11:31.524
and we moved east, here to Southern Ellesmere Island.

00:11:31.525 --> 00:11:34.660
And as soon as we did that, you can see these beautiful landscapes

00:11:34.661 --> 00:11:38.998
here, of mountains and areas. And these are all red beds that contain

00:11:38.999 --> 00:11:42.201
Devonian rocks formed in ancient rivers and streams.

00:11:42.202 --> 00:11:46.272
Took another few years, we eventually honed in on an individual

00:11:46.273 --> 00:11:49.842
valley that had good fossil preservation. And this is that valley here.

00:11:49.843 --> 00:11:53.412
What you see, in front of us, is a big hole in the middle of this

00:11:53.413 --> 00:11:57.149
slide, that shows a layer that was preserving hundreds of fossil

00:11:57.150 --> 00:12:02.255
fish, preserved one on top of the other. So, the arctic's a big place. Fossils

00:12:02.256 --> 00:12:04.357
-- you know they're not very big, so you're trying to find the little

00:12:04.358 --> 00:12:08.761
tiny patches of rock that will have those fossils inside. And what we use

00:12:08.762 --> 00:12:12.798
are the tools of geology and sedimentology. Well, we worked this layer,

00:12:12.799 --> 00:12:17.036
and everything changed one day in 2004, when my colleague,

00:12:17.037 --> 00:12:20.339
Steve Gates, he's shown in blue here to the left, removed a rock

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:24.777
from this layer. And he saw a little notch, a little v, you can see that v

00:12:24.778 --> 00:12:28.114
in the middle of this slide. If you look very carefully you can see it.

00:12:28.115 --> 00:12:31.884
He says, hey guys, what's this? We run over to it and we saw what

00:12:31.885 --> 00:12:36.055
we spent multiple years looking for and several decades of a quest. What we had

00:12:36.056 --> 00:12:40.493
looking at us here is the snout of a fish, and not just any fish, the snout

00:12:40.494 --> 00:12:43.930
of a flat headed fish. Remember I told you the conical head and the flathead

00:12:43.931 --> 00:12:47.266
was the big piece of this transition? What I had here, in the middle of this

00:12:47.267 --> 00:12:51.137
slide, was a flat headed fish sticking right out at me. With any luck at all,

00:12:51.138 --> 00:12:54.740
the rest of the fish would be preserved in the rock. So we removed

00:12:54.741 --> 00:12:58.044
this thing and they come home at the base of the helicopter, so this is a

00:12:58.045 --> 00:13:01.347
first year graduate student for scale. And it comes back several hundred

00:13:01.348 --> 00:13:07.820
miles to a supply base. And as we remove this first specimen, we

00:13:07.821 --> 00:13:11.057
found several more. So at the end of the 2004 season, we had multiple

00:13:11.058 --> 00:13:16.696
specimens of this flat headed fish. We come back to the lab, we're really

00:13:16.697 --> 00:13:19.966
excited. We come back to the lab and the preparers start removing

00:13:19.967 --> 00:13:24.637
the rock, grain by grain, to expose the fossils within. This is what

00:13:24.638 --> 00:13:28.274
Steve's specimen looked like several months after preparation.

00:13:28.275 --> 00:13:31.310
You can see what's emerging is sort of a flat head with eyes on top.

00:13:31.311 --> 00:13:35.781
You can see the tool that's used here to remove the specimen, after

00:13:35.782 --> 00:13:39.285
several more months, what's exposed. Look here we sort of have a flatheaded

00:13:39.286 --> 00:13:42.822
animal with eyes on top, even a shoulder that looks separated from

00:13:42.823 --> 00:13:46.592
the rest of the head. Remember what began this quest? In graduate

00:13:46.593 --> 00:13:49.662
school I saw this slide, fish on top, tetrapod on bottom.

00:13:49.663 --> 00:13:52.999
We wanted a flat headed fish. After several decades, using

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:56.569
tools of evolutionary biology, using tools of centrography, using the tools

00:13:56.570 --> 00:14:00.473
of geology, this is the flat headed fish. This is Steve's specimen

00:14:00.474 --> 00:14:04.310
as removed from that hole. Flathead, eyes on top, has a neck,

00:14:04.311 --> 00:14:07.580
you can see the head is separated from the shoulder, it has fins,

00:14:07.581 --> 00:14:11.551
it has fin rays, and it has scales on its back. Like a fish, it has

00:14:11.552 --> 00:14:16.455
scales and fins, like an early limbed animal, it has eyes on top,

00:14:16.456 --> 00:14:19.058
it has a neck. And indeed, when we crack open the fin, what

00:14:19.059 --> 00:14:23.296
do we find? Bones that correspond with the upper arm, forearm, and even parts

00:14:23.297 --> 00:14:26.799
of a wrist. This is a real mix of characteristics. Found at the right

00:14:26.800 --> 00:14:31.037
part of the fossil record. So this creature we call Tiktaalik, Tiktaalik

00:14:31.038 --> 00:14:34.407
roseae. And you can see here, what's sort of salient about it. It has a

00:14:34.408 --> 00:14:38.744
mix of tetrapod and fish characteristics, like a lobed fin fish it has fins,

00:14:38.745 --> 00:14:44.417
scales, and primitive jaws. Like an early tetrapod, it has a neck, a wrist,

00:14:44.418 --> 00:14:50.756
a flathead, and so forth. So here's a creature with lungs and gills, here's a

00:14:50.757 --> 00:14:53.726
creature with limbs that have limb bones in them but also fin bones,

00:14:53.727 --> 00:14:57.530
as well. Fined rays. It's really a mix between fish and tetrapod.

00:14:57.531 --> 00:15:00.867
And when we put it in the phylogenetic analysis, the evolutionary

00:15:00.868 --> 00:15:05.004
tree, you can see Tiktaalik drawn right in the middle here. It really

00:15:05.005 --> 00:15:09.275
shows a fine sequence of the emergence of tetrapod characteristics

00:15:09.276 --> 00:15:13.412
throughout the Devonian. So what's the take home message here?

00:15:13.413 --> 00:15:17.049
The take home message here is that we can predict likely and unlikely

00:15:17.050 --> 00:15:21.821
places to find fossils. We can, in doing that, learn about the great

00:15:21.822 --> 00:15:24.857
transitions in the history of life. In particular, this one, the transition

00:15:24.858 --> 00:15:30.263
from life in water to life on land. But the story doesn't stop there, it begins there.

00:15:30.264 --> 00:15:34.233
Why? Because we can trace the bones in a creature like Tiktaalik.

00:15:34.234 --> 00:15:37.503
LIke the arm bone, the upper arm bone, the humerus of Tiktaalik,

00:15:37.504 --> 00:15:40.706
we can trace it all the way to other mammals and to people.

00:15:40.707 --> 00:15:45.845
This story of the origin of the neck, the origin of a wrist, is not

00:15:45.846 --> 00:15:49.582
just some esoteric event in the history of life. It's an event

00:15:49.583 --> 00:15:53.252
embedded in our own bodies. The origin of the neck, the first time we see

00:15:53.253 --> 00:15:56.255
in Tiktaalik and its evolutionary cousins, is something that is to become

00:15:56.256 --> 00:15:59.325
our own neck. Likewise with the arm bones. So every time you

00:15:59.326 --> 00:16:02.195
shake your head, every time you bend your wrists, you can say

00:16:02.196 --> 00:16:05.698
"Thank you, Tiktaalik and its evolutionary cousins that lived in the

00:16:05.699 --> 00:16:23.684
Devonian streams 375 million years ago."

